{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A balance scale is continuous and can rest at any real-number fulcrum, whereas integer sequences are discrete.","The physical spacing of weights on a scale is arbitrary, whereas consecutive even integers have a rigid gap of exactly 2."],"overturn_conditions":["If the balance scale analogy is applied to a target sum with an odd mean (e.g., 141/3 = 47), the discrete parity mismatch invalidates the transfer.","If the required spacing between integers were anything other than 2, the symmetric counterweight model would not apply."],"shared_structure":["Both a physical balance scale and an arithmetic sequence share a center of mass (the mean) with symmetrically distributed weights around it.","In both systems, the total sum is conserved by equal and opposite displacements from the center."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003"],"transfer_claims":["Just as moving mass from one side of a fulcrum to the other conserves total weight, adding 2 to the left and subtracting 2 from the right conserves the total sum of the sequence.","The largest integer in the sequence corresponds to the lightest counterweight on the scale, which is positioned furthest from the center."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The largest even integer is 48, found by treating the sum as three weights on a balance scale: 138/3 = 46 is the fulcrum, and since the even integers are spaced in units of 2, the sequence 44, 46, 48 naturally balances to 138.","counterconditions":["If 138 were not exactly divisible by 3, the fulcrum would fall on a non-integer, preventing an integer triple.","If the problem required consecutive odd integers instead of even, the fulcrum 46 would not match the required parity, breaking the mechanism."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A causal 'counterweight' mechanism: for any balanced triple of evenly-spaced integers, the mean of the numbers must equal the middle number. Here, 138/3 = 46 is the balance point.